  [PASS] 9-isolated AGAINST INTEREST -- even the ISOLATED prediction is low.  On the isotropic Wolf estimator at the half-light radius the eleven Coma UDGs sit about 0.4 dex ABOVE the kernel's isolated prediction, all eleven with the same sign, which is a factor 1.6 in sigma.  The published resolution (Freundlich+2022) is radial anisotropy, which raises the predicted central dispersion -- a free ingredient, not a prediction.  So the framework does NOT put these galaxies on the relation without help, and the widely quoted 'UDGs lie on the RAR' is a statement about the RAR's scatter at accelerations 1.5 dex below any rotation-curve data   (canonical mean offset +0.396 +- 0.062 dex (6.4 sigma on the quoted errors); alt +0.357 +- 0.062 dex (5.8 sigma); all 11/11 offsets have the same sign; y_int ~ 0.004-0.02, i.e. deep MOND)
  [PASS] 9-EFE THE KILL FIRES, and it is recorded as a LIABILITY.  Switching on the external-field effect -- which the framework does not get to decline, it is a consequence of the nonlinear field equation -- suppresses the predicted dispersions and TRIPLES the deficit above, from 0.4 dex to 1.2 dex.  This is Freundlich+2022's result reproduced independently on Route A and on both footings, and it is the framework's own strong-equivalence-principle violation failing where it should be easiest to see   (canonical mean offset WITH the EFE +1.195 +- 0.062 dex = 19.4 sigma (a factor 15.7 in acceleration, 4.0 in sigma); alt +1.166 (18.9 sigma). Note this used nu(x_ext), the LARGEST EFE eigenvalue, i.e. the most generous possible treatment)
  [PASS] 9-diagnostic ...and the EFE offset tracks the external field itself: the UDGs nearest the cluster centre, where x_ext is largest, are the worst off.  That is the signature of the EFE being the cause, not of a random mass-to-light problem, so the liability cannot be argued away as scatter   (d log(g_obs/g_EFE)/d log x_ext = +0.68 over x_ext = 0.30-0.85)
  the escape routes, listed so they can be tested rather than assumed (they are Freundlich+2022's own): a higher
  baryonic mass than the spectra give; tidal heating inflating sigma; or the EFE being SCREENED inside clusters --
  which would be the same unexplained cluster-scale effect that items 7, 10 and 18 in this pass keep finding.
  What is NOT available is making the EFE weaker: nu(x_ext) already is its most generous form.
  [PASS] M9 mutation: with nu = 1 (no modification) the SAME data show a factor-25 discrepancy, so the kernel is what puts these galaxies on the relation in the first place -- the isolated pass above is not trivially true   (nu = 1 leaves a median log(g_obs/g_bar) = 1.39 dex (a factor 25); the kernel leaves +0.40 dex)
  sensitivity to the Coma mass model: scaling g_ext by 0.5x moves the EFE offset from +1.195 to +1.086 dex -- the liability does not depend on the cluster model
  sensitivity to the Coma mass model: scaling g_ext by 2.0x moves the EFE offset from +1.195 to +1.289 dex -- the liability does not depend on the cluster model

